lation.\",\n    \"abstraction_for_handoff\": \"How do you guarantee a small trusted subset of items is preserved when the governing policy excludes the containers those items live inside?\"\n  },\n  \"reasoning\": \"The project is a correctly-functioning bootstrap: register -> create isolated per-project namespace (.anchor/projects/<id>/) -> scan -> emit a structure-only Anchor.md and a discovery.json index. As a registration step it behaves as designed and the empty-lane state is expected, not a defect. The interesting load-bearing claim is the tracking policy in .anchor/README + .anchor/.gitignore, which asserts it reconciles C9 (durable, git-trackable pointer-records survive restart) with C10 (no personal-data / absolute-path leak; distribution scan refuses to ship anything under .anchor/). Two latent gaps sit underneath that claim. (1) discovery.json contains an ABSOLUTE Windows path in its `root` field (and is positioned to carry discovered doc paths/titles), yet it matches no ignore rule (it is not under jobs/, not *.log/*.pdf/raw/) and is not on the pointer-record re-include allow-list — so a `git add .anchor/` tracks it by default, committing exactly the 'absolute paths' the README names as the C10 threat. (2) The re-include negations `!projects/*/**/index.json` and `!projects/*/**/*.pointer.json` are INERT for any pointer-record nested under a directory-excluded container — git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so `projects/*/jobs/` and `projects/*/**/raw/` swallow any index/pointer record placed inside them, silently breaking C9 durability for those records. A verdict-inverting reading tempers (1): the Wave-8 distribution scan reportedly 'refuses to ship anything under .anchor/', so if that scan holds, the leak blast-radius is the developer's own git history and collaborators, not the public distro — real, but narrower than a public leak. Reason precedes verdict: the scaffold is sound as a bootstrap; the two findings are latent policy gaps, not present-state breakage of this empty fixture.\",\n  \"verdict\": \"Sound as a freshly-registered bootstrap scaffold, with two latent C9/C10 policy defects in the git-tracking design that will bite once real efforts and sharing occur. Not 'fully sound' — Channel-2 defects are present.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"discovery.json holds `\\\"root\\\": \\\"C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\john\\\\\\\\AppData\\\\\\\\Local\\\\\\\\Temp\\\\\\\\...\\\\\\\\registered\\\"` — an absolute user path. .anchor/README's C10 clause names 'absolute paths' as precisely what must be git-IGNORED, but no rule in .anchor/.gitignore matches discovery.json (it is not under jobs/, not *.log/*.pdf, not under raw/), and it is not on the `!...index.json`/`!...*.pointer.json` re-include allow-list. Net: under the policy's own intended `git add .anchor/`, discovery.json is TRACKED and commits the absolute path. This is the OPERATOR-WRONG finding: the policy the author is evidently proud of (reconciling durability with no-leak) leaves its own scan-output file unclassified and leaking the exact data class it set out to contain.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"C10 no-leak is violated for discovery.json as written; the file should be classified (ignored, or `root` redacted/relativized).\",\n      \"inverting_reading\": \"If the Wave-8 distribution scan truly excludes ALL of .anchor/ from any shipped distro, the leak is confined to local git history + collaborators, not the public distribution — this narrows severity from 'public leak' to 'repo-history disclosure', but does not eliminate it (collaborators and forks still receive the absolute path).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The re-include negations `!projects/*/**/index.json` and `!projects/*/**/*.pointer.json` cannot rescue any pointer-record placed inside a directory-excluded container. git's documented limitation: a file cannot be re-included if a parent directory of it is excluded. `projects/*/jobs/` and `projects/*/**/raw/` are directory-level exclusions, so an index.json/pointer.json written under e.g. `projects/<id>/build/jobs/<run>/index.json` is silently NOT tracked, defeating C9 durability for exactly the records the policy promises to preserve. (Note: the `*.log`/`*.pdf` rules are file-globs, not dir exclusions, so negations would work there — the hazard is specific to jobs/ and raw/.)\",\n      \"verdict\": \"C9 durability has a silent failure mode for pointer-records nested under jobs/ or raw/; either keep pointer-records out of those containers by construction, or replace the directory exclusions with nested file-glob exclusions so the negations remain effective.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"nitpick\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"discovery.json enumerates FIVE lanes — research, planning, build, deliverables, AND `grass` — in both `by_lane` and `counts`, but the generated, human-facing Anchor.md documents only FOUR lanes and never mentions `grass`. The structure-only summary the author points humans at omits a real lane present in the machine index.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Either surface `grass` in Anchor.md generation or document why it is intentionally hidden; the divergence will confuse a reader who trusts Anchor.md as the structure of record.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"A1\",\n      \"kind\": \"anticipate\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"future_state_condition\": \"Several months in, this project has accumulated real build efforts, and on a fresh clone / post-restart the team finds part of the effort history is missing — specific pointer-records that should index past runs are simply absent from git, even though the .anchor README promised they are durable.\",\n      \"enabling_assumption\": \"A lane writer places its index.json / *.pointer.json INSIDE a blanket-excluded container — e.g. `projects/<id>/build/jobs/<run>/index.json` or anything under a `raw/` subtree — trusting the `!...index.json` negation to re-include it, unaware that git cannot re-include under an excluded directory. The record is silently never committed; the gap is invisible until a clone/restart reveals it.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the forward (cardinality-1) projection of F2: the durability claim is only as strong as the directory in which writers happen to drop records, and nothing in the policy enforces that placement.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Pre-empt by either (a) mandating pointer-records at lane root only, or (b) using nested file-glob exclusions instead of directory exclusions so negations survive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"A2\",\n      \"kind\": \"anticipate\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"future_state_condition\": \"Months later this project is shared or distributed to a collaborator/fork, and the recipient's clone contains discovery.json carrying the original author's absolute home/temp paths (and the titles/relative locations of every discovered artifact) — the precise personal-data disclosure the C10 policy and Wave-8 scan were created to prevent.\",\n      \"enabling_assumption\": \"discovery.json is committed (it matches no ignore rule and is not on the re-include allow-list) AND the distribution/no-leak guard keys on a path-prefix or rule-set that this particular file evades — so the file rides along into shared artifacts/history even though the policy's intent was to strip absolute paths.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Forward (cardinality-1) projection of F1: the leak is latent and zero-impact while the repo is private and empty, but compounds the moment the durability mechanism does its job (committing .anchor contents) and the project is shared.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Pre-empt by classifying discovery.json under C10 now (ignore it or redact `root`), and by making the no-leak guard scan file CONTENTS for absolute paths, not just path prefixes.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nitpicks\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"N1\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"discovery.json stores `generated_at` as a raw float Unix epoch (1782259380.78) while Anchor.md renders the same scan time as a human string '2026-06-23 18:03:00'. Two representations of one fact invite drift; consider deriving both from a single source field.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"N2\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"readme.txt contains only the placeholder 'hi'. Harmless in a fixture, but if this scaffolds real projects, a non-placeholder template (or omission) reads more intentionally.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"elevations\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E1\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"high\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Classify discovery.json explicitly under C10: add it to .anchor/.gitignore (or relativize/redact its `root` to a project-relative marker) so the scan-output index never commits an absolute path. This closes the F1/A2 leak at its source rather than relying on a downstream distribution scan that the file currently evades. Cheap, local, and directly upholds the policy's stated intent.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Recommended forward fix for the no-leak (C10) gap.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If discovery.json is in fact NEVER git-committed in practice — i.e. the registration flow only ever commits Anchor.md and the explicit pointer-records, and an existing pre-commit/Wave-8 guard already strips or excludes discovery.json (not merely 'anything under .anchor' as an aspiration but a verified, tested exclusion of this file) — then there is no live leak and E1 is redundant.\",\n      \"refutation_provenance\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E2\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"high\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Make C9 durability robust against placement: either (a) require pointer-records (index.json / *.pointer.json) to live ONLY at a lane root that is never inside an excluded container, enforced by the writer, or (b) replace the directory-level exclusions `projects/*/jobs/` and `projects/*/**/raw/` with nested file-glob exclusions (e.g. ignore `projects/*/**/jobs/**` by file type) so the `!...index.json` / `!...*.pointer.json` negations remain effective. Either removes the silent-drop failure mode in F2/A1.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Recommended forward fix for the durability (C9) gap.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If, by design and enforcement, no pointer-record is EVER written under a jobs/ or raw/ subtree (lane writers are structurally constrained to place index.json at `projects/<id>/<lane>/index.json` and nowhere nested), then git's re-include-under-excluded-directory limitation never triggers and E2 addresses a path that cannot occur.\",\n      \"refutation_provenance\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E3\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"low\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reconcile the lane vocabulary (F3): have Anchor.md generation surface (or explicitly footnote) the `grass` lane present in discovery.json, so the human structure-of-record matches the machine index. Improves trust in Anchor.md as the canonical structural view.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Minor consistency improvement.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If `grass` is an intentional internal/uncategorized bucket deliberately excluded from the human-facing summary by design, then omitting it from Anchor.md is correct and E3 is unwanted.\",\n      \"refutation_provenance\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"honest_stamps\": {\n    \"substrate\": \"single-family (cross_model:false) — refuter/judge would be a conservative lower bound; GROUNDED is unreachable on this host, max achievable is PROMISING and only for above-threshold elevations that an independent refuter actually fails to land.\",\n    \"runtime_scope\": 